     1	// lib/python/bytes_repr.mjs
     2	//
     3	// Python `repr()` formatters, driven by explicit lookup tables that map 1:1 onto
     4	// the MEASURED ground-truth table in the porting spec. Nothing here is clever:
     5	// every rule is a table row you can read next to the spec.
     6	//
     7	// Two formatters live here because they share one piece of machinery -- Python's
     8	// quote-selection rule -- and nothing else:
     9	//   * pyBytesRepr(byteArray) -> repr() of a `bytes` object   (used by print(data))
    10	//   * pyStrRepr(text)        -> repr() of a `str` object     (used by argparse's
    11	//                               "invalid int value: %r" message)
    12	//
    13	// The built byte sequence is a PLAIN Array of integers 0..255. No binary-view or
    14	// typed-sequence APIs of any kind are used; the repr is formatted straight from
    15	// that plain array.
    16	
    17	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    18	// Quote selection (MEASURED)
    19	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    20	// Python picks the quote character before it escapes anything:
    21	//
    22	//   contains '  | contains "  | quote used | notes
    23	//   -----------+--------------+------------+---------------------------------
    24	//   no         | no           | '          | nothing special
    25	//   no         | yes          | '          | the " stays literal
    26	//   yes        | no           | "          | the ' stays literal (b"'A")
    27	//   yes        | yes          | '          | the ' is escaped as \' (b'\'"')
